Scenario: you must migrate a legacy plain-text contact file into a structured CSV with exactly three columns: name, email, phone. The legacy file mixes line formats (some lines have a missing phone, some have extra whitespace, and a few phone numbers are stored as digits-only). You cannot execute the migration here; your job is to produce a WRITTEN plan only. Write a plan that contains ALL of the following, labelled:
(1) a list of 5-9 concrete steps in a sensible order, from reading the source file to writing the final CSV;
(2) an explicit statement of how you will handle the specific dirty-data cases named above (missing phone, padded whitespace, digits-only phone) — state the transformer rule for each;
(3) a stated 'done' criterion (what must be true of the output for the migration to be complete);
(4) an explicit verification step (e.g., a row-count check that the CSV has no fewer rows than source lines minus known-invalid records, and a sample spot-check).

The function below is supposed to group words into anagrams (words that are rearrangements of each other) and return a list of groups, in the order each group FIRST appears in the input. Running it currently raises an exception. Fix ONLY `group_anagrams` in `fix_anagrams.py`; do not change the function name or signature.

def group_anagrams(words):
    groups = {}
    for w in words:
        key = sorted(w)
        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(w)
    return list(groups.values())

After fixing, append and run these assertions; all must pass:
assert sorted(l) for l in group_anagrams(['eat','tea','tan','ate','nat','bat']) groups all the same letters
assert sum(len(g) for g in group_anagrams(['eat','tea','tan','ate','nat','bat'])) == 6
assert group_anagrams([]) == []
assert group_anagrams(['a']) == [['a']]
assert group_anagrams(['ab','ba','ab']) == [['ab','ba','ab']]  # order within a group preserved
